From 0f68ed5f5281ad9c6045b17a38cfc6f96d4804b9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: izigower <hugokemaies@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 20:43:23 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] elan: retry on transient not-ready status for all devices

CAPTURE_READ_DATA only allows a retry when the sensor answers 0x00 (not
ready) or 0xaf (busy) if dev_type is ELAN_0C58. Every other device in the
table gets FP_DEVICE_ERROR_PROTO instead, which aborts enrollment.

Those two values describe a transient state rather than a device-specific
quirk, and at least 04f3:0c6e reports them as well while the sensor
settles after activation. On that sensor enrollment always failed:

  [elan] CAPTURE_NUM_STATES entering state 2
  [elan] SSM CAPTURE_NUM_STATES failed in state 2 with error:
         The driver encountered a protocol error with the device.

libfprint-image_device also warned about the resulting illegal
transition from AWAIT_FINGER_ON to AWAIT_FINGER_OFF, repeating every ten
seconds without ever reaching CAPTURE.

Dropping the model check lets any sensor reporting those states retry, as
0x0c58 already did. Enrollment now completes on 04f3:0c6e (ASUS ZenBook
14X OLED UX5400EA); 0x0c58 behaviour is unchanged.

Signed-off-by: izigower <hugokemaies@gmail.com>
---
 libfprint/drivers/elan.c | 9 +++++++--
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/libfprint/drivers/elan.c b/libfprint/drivers/elan.c
index f9cc52f..0ac5e9a 100644
--- a/libfprint/drivers/elan.c
+++ b/libfprint/drivers/elan.c
@@ -557,11 +557,16 @@ capture_run_state (FpiSsm *ssm, FpDevice *dev)
           fpi_image_device_report_finger_status (idev, TRUE);
           elan_run_cmd (ssm, dev, &get_image_cmd, ELAN_CMD_TIMEOUT);
         }
-      else if (self->dev_type == ELAN_0C58 && self->last_read &&
+      else if (self->last_read &&
                (self->last_read[0] == 0x00 || self->last_read[0] == 0xaf))
         {
           /* 0x00 - not ready
-           * 0xaf - finger removed or sensor busy (seen on 0x0c58) */
+           * 0xaf - finger removed or sensor busy
+           *
+           * These are transient states, not errors. They were first seen on
+           * 0x0c58, but 0x0c6e reports them too while the sensor settles.
+           * Retrying regardless of the model avoids turning a normal "not
+           * ready yet" answer into a fatal protocol error. */
           fpi_ssm_jump_to_state_delayed (ssm, CAPTURE_WAIT_FINGER, 10);
         }
       else
